## Purpose

Keep commits/PRs **clean and intentional** (RN/Expo, Expo Router). This rule runs on **every task** and the agent decides when to commit.

## Non‑negotiables (run before planning commits/PR)

```bash
git status
git diff
git diff --staged
git diff --stat
git log --oneline --decorate -n 30
git branch --show-current
```

Then write a **3–7 bullet summary**:
- 2–4 user‑facing bullets
- 1–3 technical bullets

## Safety: never commit secrets

Before staging, scan diffs for secrets and **refuse to commit** if any are found.

Examples of sensitive data:
- Passwords, API keys, auth tokens, session cookies
- Bitcoin seed phrases / mnemonics (12–24 words), xprv, WIF, raw private keys
- Nostr secrets (`nsec1...`), NIP-44 conversation keys, hex private keys
- `.env` contents / credential files

If suspected:
- Remove from diff, rotate the secret, and add/confirm `.gitignore` entries.
- If a secret was committed, immediately rewrite history and force‑push with a clear note.

## Branch rules (always)

- **Never commit to `main`/`master`**. If currently on it:

```bash
git checkout -b <type>/<scope>-<short-kebab>
```

- Branch name may change as scope evolves:

```bash
git branch -m <type>/<scope>-<short-kebab>
```

## Commit decision (no user confirmation)

Commit if any are true:
- UI/user behavior changes
- Non‑trivial fix/perf/refactor
- New files, risky logic (payments/keys/storage/networking)
- Tooling/config/patches/lockfiles changed

Skip commit only if truly trivial or user explicitly says “no commits”.

## Commit & scope conventions

- Conventional Commits: `type(scope): subject` (≤72 chars, imperative, no period)
- If the subject line isn’t enough, it’s valid to add a **commit description/body** (multiple lines) to capture extra context (why, tradeoffs, edge cases). Keep the subject concise; put the longer explanation in the body.
- Allowed `type`: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `docs`, `style`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`
- Pick **one primary scope**. Prefer deriving scope from the **diff paths**, then feature intent.
  - Examples: `app/(mint-flow)` → `mint`, `app/(drawer)` → `nav`/tab scope, `components/ui` → `ui`, `patches/*` → `patches`, `metro.config.js` → `metro`

## Commit plan & staging

- Plan 2–6 commits when changes are non‑trivial; keep deps/lockfiles/patches separate.
- Stage precisely (`git add -p`) and avoid committing broken states.
- If already pushed and you must rewrite: use `git push --force-with-lease` and report it.

## Validation (only claim what you ran)

Before **any commit**, ensure the full “quality gate” passes locally (match CI):
- `npm run lint`
- `npm run type-check`
- `npm run pretty:check`
- `npm run knip`

If any fail:
- Fix, then re-run the same commands.
- If formatting is the issue, run `npm run pretty` (write), then re-run `npm run pretty:check`.

If validation fails: fix and `git commit --amend` the relevant commit.

## PR creation (squash‑merge ready)

- PR title = final Conventional Commit subject
- PR body template:

```text
What / Why
- …

What changed
- …

Screenshots / Video
- N/A

Test plan
1) …

Notes / Risks
- …
```

Use `gh pr create/edit` and push branch (`git push -u origin <branch>`).